  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L04B_004_Greet With String Hints",
   "display_name": "Greet With String Hints",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Type hints work for <code>str</code> too - and here's a good chance to remember the difference between a function that <b>returns</b> a value and one that <b>prints</b> it.</p>\n<pre>def greet(name: str) -> str:\n    return \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\"</pre>\n<p>Notice this function <b>returns</b> a string - it does <b>not</b> call <code>print()</code> itself. The caller decides what to do with the returned value, including printing it if they want to.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Complete the function <code>greet</code> below (its type-hinted signature is already provided) so that it <b>returns</b> the string <code>Hello, &lt;name&gt;!</code> (do not print inside the function).</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>greet(\"Alice\")</code> should return <code>\"Hello, Alice!\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def greet(name: str) -> str:\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(greet(\"Alice\"))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(greet(\"Alice\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Hello, Alice!",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(greet(\"Bob\"))",
