  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L04B_005_Is Even With Bool Hint",
   "display_name": "Is Even With Bool Hint",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Python also has a <code>bool</code> type, for values that are either <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> - exactly what a condition like <code>n % 2 == 0</code> evaluates to. Type hints can use <code>bool</code> just like any other type.</p>\n<pre>def is_even(n: int) -> bool:\n    return n % 2 == 0</pre>\n<p>Here, the return type <code>-&gt; bool</code> tells us this function always returns <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>, never a number or string.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Complete the function <code>is_even</code> below (its type-hinted signature is already provided) so that it returns <code>True</code> if <code>n</code> is even, or <code>False</code> if it is odd.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>is_even(4)</code> should return <code>True</code>. <code>is_even(7)</code> should return <code>False</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def is_even(n: int) -> bool:\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(is_even(4))",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(is_even(4))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(is_even(7))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "False",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(is_even(0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
