   "display_name": "Add Hints To Upper Greeting",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Below is a function that already works correctly, but its signature has <b>no type hints</b> at all.</p>\n<pre>def upper_greeting(name):\n    return \"HELLO \" + name.upper()</pre>\n<p>(Here, <code>.upper()</code> is a small built-in string tool that converts text to uppercase - e.g. <code>\"bob\".upper()</code> gives <code>\"BOB\"</code>.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Rewrite the signature of <code>upper_greeting</code> to add the correct type hints - the parameter is meant to be <code>str</code>, and the function returns a <code>str</code>. <b>Do not change the function's logic</b> - only add the type hints to the <code>def</code> line.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>upper_greeting(\"bob\")</code> should still return <code>\"HELLO BOB\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def upper_greeting(name):\n    return \"HELLO \" + name.upper()\n\n# ---- Your task: rewrite the def line above to add type hints, then keep the same body ----\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(upper_greeting(\"bob\"))",
